---
import LegacyLayout from '@/layouts/LegacyLayout.astro';
import FoundationalReading from '@/components/legacy/FoundationalReading.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/site-config';
---

<LegacyLayout
  title="Research"
  subPage="research"
  eyebrow="Research abstract, materials, and contact for graduate researchers"
  description="Developmental Theatre is a fifty-year lineage waiting for a definitive history. This page is for the researcher who will write it."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-10">

    {/* CLIENT REVIEW: abstract text below is the Cycle 5 draft carried forward from history.astro.
        Needs reconciliation with Doc #10 (Research Abstract) verbatim once client provides the source. */}
    <section>
      <h2>Research abstract</h2>
      <p>
        Developmental Theatre began in 1971 at the University of Colorado when Richard Knaub,
        Martin Cobin, Chuck Wilcox, and Lola Wilcox launched the Colorado Caravan under NEA
        Title III funding. The Caravan&rsquo;s bare-stage, workshop-forward touring model
        &mdash; five or six actors, one van, gymnasiums and cafetoriums in-the-round, four age
        levels served, an interactive workshop after every performance &mdash; proved
        replicable. Its first offshoot, the Overland Stage Company, launched in Wyoming in
        1978; similar companies followed in Maine, Montana, Hawaii, Denmark, and Australia.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        Across more than fifty years, the Caravan and its successor companies have performed
        for over six million people on three continents. The M.A. programme in Developmental
        Theatre at CU, led by Dr. Martin Cobin, institutionalised the practice inside a
        university department for the first time and launched careers that have carried
        Developmental Theatre&rsquo;s tools into schools, community theatres, universities,
        and therapeutic settings around the world. The programme and the Caravan itself
        ceased when their institutional sponsors moved on. The practice they developed did
        not: DT:FC is the current continuation.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        A definitive history of Developmental Theatre has not yet been written. The
        materials to write one exist, are accessible, and are catalogued below. We are
        actively seeking a graduate researcher to take on that project.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Research materials</h2>
      <p>
        Materials pertinent to gathering qualitative data sets on Developmental Theatre&rsquo;s
        history, methods, and outcomes:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Participant contacts</strong> &mdash; contact information for
          actors, directors, teachers, and audience members across more than ten years of
          active touring, plus alumni of the CU M.A. programme.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Physical evidence</strong> &mdash; scripts, reviews, scholarly papers,
          programmes, correspondence with local sponsors, and videos and drawings from
          the touring years.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developmental Theatre Facebook Group</strong> &mdash; an active
          community where alumni post recollections, share archival photos, and connect
          across generations of the practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Master Timeline with replication branches</strong> &mdash; the
          <a href="/legacy/timeline/" class="hover:text-teal-600">Grand Timeline</a> traces
          the Caravan and its successor companies in Wyoming, Maine, Montana, Hawaii,
          Denmark, and Australia.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bios</strong> &mdash; a growing archive linking Developmental Theatre
          training with lifelong careers in performance, education, and community
          practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Script, workshop, and competency synopsis</strong> &mdash; a working
          document mapping the plays, workshops, and skills the Caravan developed and
          taught across its touring life and its M.A. programme.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Foundational reading</h2>
      <p>
        The theoretical and practical texts the Caravan&rsquo;s founders drew on as
        they built the practice. The list is short by design &mdash; these six works
        anchor most of Developmental Theatre&rsquo;s vocabulary.
      </p>
      <FoundationalReading />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        Interested in researching this history? Contact us. We can put you in touch with
        living participants, share the physical archive, and help you scope a project that
        fits your programme or your timeline.
      </p>
      <div class="border-ink-500/25 bg-ink-500/5 mt-4 rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border p-6">
        <p class="text-ink-700 text-sm">
          Research inquiries currently route by email. Please write to{' '}
          <a
            href={`mailto:${SITE_CONFIG.fallbackContactEmail}?subject=Research%20inquiry%20%E2%80%94%20Developmental%20Theatre`}
            class="hover:text-teal-600 underline"
          >
            {SITE_CONFIG.fallbackContactEmail}
          </a>{' '}
          with a short description of your programme, your research question, and your
          timeline &mdash; we&rsquo;ll respond within a week.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</LegacyLayout>
